   "item": {
    "subject_key": "ap-chemistry",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.2",
    "topic_title": "Mass Spectra of Elements",
    "stem": "A mass spectrum of a pure sample of element X shows only two peaks, both from singly charged monatomic ions. One peak is at 10.0 amu with a relative intensity of 25. The other is at 11.0 amu with a relative intensity of 100. The instrument scales the tallest peak to 100. What is the average atomic mass of X?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "10.5 amu",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This is an unweighted average, (10.0 + 11.0)/2, which treats both isotopes as equally common. The 11.0 amu isotope is four times as abundant, so it must pull the average well above the midpoint. Fix: Convert each peak height to a fractional abundance and multiply each mass by its fraction before adding."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "11.0 amu",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This reports only the mass of the tallest peak, as if the most abundant isotope alone set the atomic mass. The 10.0 amu isotope makes up 20% of the sample and lowers the average below 11.0. Fix: Include every peak in the sum, weighting each isotope mass by its fractional abundance."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "13.5 amu",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This uses the scaled heights 0.25 and 1.00 as if they were fractions, giving 2.5 + 11.0. Those numbers sum to 1.25, not 1, so they are not true fractional abundances. A weighted average can never exceed the heaviest isotope's mass. Fix: Divide each peak height by the total of all heights so the fractions sum to 1."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "10.8 amu",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "The intensities total 125, so the fractional abundances are 25/125 = 0.20 and 100/125 = 0.80. Weighting each isotope mass by its fraction gives (10.0)(0.20) + (11.0)(0.80) = 2.0 + 8.8 = 10.8 amu. This value lies closer to 11.0 because that isotope is more abundant."
     }
    ]
   },
